STM32 Programmable Sine Wave Generator

Project Overview
Developed a programmable sine-wave generator using an STM32 NUCLEO-F446ZE microcontroller
as part of the CPEG222 Embedded Systems course at the University of Delaware.
The system uses embedded C programming and STM32 hardware peripherals to generate a sine-wave
output with an adjustable frequency ranging from 100 Hz to 2,000 Hz.

Technologies Used:
Microcontroller: STM32 NUCLEO-F446ZE
Programming Language: Embedded C
Development Environment: VS Code, PlatformIO
Hardware Peripherals: ADC, DAC, DMA, TIM5, TIM6, GPIO
Display: Four-digit seven-segment display

Key Features:
Adjustable sine-wave frequency from 100 Hz to 2,000 Hz
Potentiometer-controlled frequency selection using a 12-bit ADC
DAC and DMA integration for waveform generation
Timer-controlled sine-wave output
Real-time frequency display on a four-digit seven-segment display
Push-button control for enabling and disabling the amplifier

How It Works:
1. Frequency Control

The potentioreter provides an analog voltage that is converted into a digital value using
the STM32's 12-bit ADC.
The ADC reading is mapped to a frequency between 100 Hz and 2,000 Hz.

2. Waveform Generation
A sine-wave lookup table contains sampled values of a sine function.
DMA transfers these samples to the DAC, while TIM6 controls the sample update rate
to produce a continuous periodic waveform.
3. Frequency Display
The selected frequency is displayed on a four-digit seven-segment display.
TIM5 periodically refreshes the display using multiplexing, allowing the four digits to appear illuminated simultaneously.

4. User Controls
The potentiometer adjusts the output frequency, while a push button controls the amplifier.
Engineering Concepts Applied
Register-level microcontroller programming
Analog-to-digital and digital-to-analog conversion
Direct Memory Access (DMA)
Hardware timer configuration
Embedded system interrupts
Seven-segment display multiplexing
Digital signal generation

